LASSO

LASSO stands for Least Absolute Shrinkage and Selection Operator. It's a type of regularised linear regression.
Regular linear regression fits:
  Y = b0 + b1*P1 + b2*P2 + ... + bn*Pn
by minimising the sum of squared errors. With 300 parameters and only
132 profiles it would wildly overfit — more unknowns than equations!

LASSO adds a penalty on the size of the coefficients:
minimise:  sum(Y - Y_pred)² + λ * sum(|bi|)

The λ * sum(|bi|) term forces coefficients to be small. The magic is
that LASSO tends to push unimportant coefficients to exactly zero —
effectively removing those parameters entirely. This is what makes it
a feature selector.

The λ parameter controls sparsity:

Small λ → many non-zero coefficients (less regularisation, like regular regression)
Large λ → few non-zero coefficients (more regularisation, more aggressive selection)
Cross-validation picks the best λ automatically

A concrete example for your problem:

Y = b1*X + b2*wavenumber + b3*P1 + b4*P2 + ... + b300*P300
LASSO might return:
b1=0.98, b2=0.15, b7=0.23, b45=0.11, all others=0
Telling you only X, wavenumber, P7 and P45 matter — ignore the other 296!

The key limitation — it's linear. So it will miss the
sigmoid/nonlinear relationships you described for the tropospheric
parameters. But it's still very useful as a first pass screen to
eliminate obviously useless parameters before feeding the survivors
into the DNN.

